      // [src_nitobe_suzuki, src_nitobe_ubc, src_nitobe_twcu | 1880s-1923 | scholarly essay + memorial garden + founder institution] Suzuki quotes his reported wish, as a young man, to 'introduce foreign thoughts to Japan and Japanese thoughts to the foreign countries', UBC says he dedicated his life to being 'a bridge across the Pacific', and the TWCU essay quotes his first-graduation speech on valuing character and 'cultivating persons rather than human resources'. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): reviewed as a top-risk row; lowered from 70 / 0.55 to 66 / 0.5 because the stated aim comes from his own reported words relayed by one chapter and by two commemorative institutions; the work is documented but his motive is not independently shown.
