    sources: [{ id: "src_ngugi_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o (Q302910) -- verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-10-26): English label \"Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o\", description 'Kenyan writer (1938-2025)', Korean label '응구기 와 시옹오', born 1938-01-05, died 2025-05-28, citizenship Kenya, P18 image 'Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o 2019 (48139052733).jpg'. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q302910" }, { id: "src_ngugi_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Ngugi wa Thiong'o' (biography entry), read in full. CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: his education at Makerere and Leeds, Weep Not, Child (1964) as the first major novel in English by an East African, A Grain of Wheat (1967), Petals of Blood (1977), Devil on the Cross (1980) in Kikuyu, Wizard of the Crow (2004), the plays co-written with Micere Githae Mugo and Ngugi wa Mirii, the essay collections including Decolonising the Mind (1986) arguing for African-language literature, and the memoirs. Reference biography: authoritative on works and chronology; the detention and exile passages were read but are political context and not scored.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Ngugi-wa-Thiongo" }, { id: "src_ngugi_ap_2025", kind: "press", title: "Hillel Italie, Associated Press, 'Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o, Kenyan dissident and author who was giant of global literature, dies at 87' (PBS NewsHour, 28 May 2025), read in full: the obituary frames his novels, memoirs and the critique Decolonizing the Mind, his place as a perennial Nobel candidate, the 2009 Booker shortlist, the 2012 National Book Critics Circle finalist and the 2016 Pak Kyong-ni Literature Award, and a photo caption stating that Wizard of the Crow took more than six years to write. Wire-service obituary; admiring in tone.", url: "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/amp/arts/ngugi-wa-thiongo-kenyan-dissident-and-author-who-was-giant-of-global-literature-dies-at-87" }, { id: "src_ngugi_leeds_2025", kind: "institution", title: "University of Leeds, Faculty of Arts, Humanities and Cultures, 'Commemorating Leeds alumnus Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o' (30 May 2025, by Brendon Nicholls), read in full: he wrote A Grain of Wheat while a Leeds student, produced eight novels, three short story collections, four children's books, five memoirs, eleven non-fiction books and five plays, championed writing in African languages and multi-centred translation, increasingly foregrounded Gikuyu oral literature, story, proverbs and song, and held a distinguished professorship at the University of California, Irvine. University obituary by a scholar; admiring, and the university awarded him an honorary doctorate in 2004.", url: "https://ahc.leeds.ac.uk/media/news/article/2916/commemorating-leeds-alumnus-ng-g-wa-thiong-o" }, { id: "src_ngugi_foundation_bio", kind: "institution", title: "Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o Foundation, 'Bio' (advocacy organisation for African languages bearing his name), read in full: his schooling, the 1962 Black Hermit performance, the shift in form with A Grain of Wheat, his work at the University of Nairobi and the jointly authored 'On the Abolition of the English Department', his Gikuyu novels, his academic posts at Yale, New York University and the University of California Irvine, his editing of the journals Penpoint, Zuka, Ghala and Mutiiri, and his lectures. AFFILIATED SOURCE: written for the foundation he is associated with, so used for chronology and activities and not as sole support for any row; the account of threats against him was read and not used.", url: "https://www.ngugiwathiongofoundation.org/bio" }, { id: "src_ngugi_gasser_2021", kind: "press", title: "Lucy Gasser, 'Post/colonial English: The language of African literature?' (poco.lit., 19 February 2021), read in full: a scholar's essay setting Ngũgĩ's Decolonising the Mind position against Chinua Achebe's pragmatic defence of English as a literary medium, the 1962 Makerere conference, the question of audience and publication reach, and the observation that English-language writing is more likely to reach a wide audience. INDEPENDENT SCHOLARLY COUNTER-VOICE: sympathetic to his arguments but presents the opposing Achebe position and the audience trade-off.", url: "https://pocolit.com/en/2021/02/19/post-colonial-english-the-language-of-african-literature/" }, { id: "src_ngugi_nation_2018", kind: "press", title: "Rohit Inani, 'Language Is a War Zone: A Conversation with Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o' (The Nation, 2018), read in full: his own account of writing for Gikuyu readers, why he held that English writing reaches people only by trickle-down, his remarks that he likes Naipaul's novels and respects Achebe's contribution while disagreeing that writing in English advances African languages. SELF MATERIAL (interview): used only as corroboration alongside non-self sources, never as sole support for any row.", url: "https://www.thenation.com/article/archive/language-is-a-war-zone-a-conversation-with-ngugi-wa-thiongo/tnamp/" }, { id: "src_ngugi_portrait", kind: "archive", title: "Wikimedia Commons file page for the 9 May 2019 photograph by Shawn Miller of the Library of Congress (Flickr account 'Library of Congress Life'), and the live Flickr photo page, checked for the portrait only.", url: "https://flickr.com/photos/162991176@N03/48139052733" }],
