      persistence: [70, 0.58, "d", "A"],
      // [src_ngugi_britannica, src_ngugi_leeds_2025, src_ngugi_foundation_bio | 1967-2004 | reference biography + university obituary + affiliated bio] The Foundation bio describes A Grain of Wheat as replacing linear narrative with multiple narrative lines and viewpoints; Britannica describes Devil on the Cross as written to recall traditional ballad singers and Wizard of the Crow as joining fantasy and satire; Leeds says he contributed key innovations in African literary form by foregrounding Gikuyu oral literature, story, proverbs and song. Formal inventiveness is documented, but the strongest judgements come from admiring or affiliated sources. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (lead review, before eligibility): creative_originality lowered from 74 / 0.64 to 72 / 0.6 because the sources are an AP obituary, a university obituary and the affiliated foundation, with no hostile critic of his literary quality read.
      creative_originality: [72, 0.6, "d", "A"],
      // [src_ngugi_leeds_2025, src_ngugi_britannica, src_ngugi_foundation_bio | 1962-2012 | university obituary + reference biography + affiliated bio] He worked across novels, plays, short stories, children's books, memoirs and critical essays (Leeds counts eight novels, five plays, five memoirs and eleven non-fiction books), edited literary journals, studied film in Stockholm and taught comparative literature and performance studies. Range across literary forms and into scholarship is well documented; it is a breadth of literary and academic work, not of unrelated fields.
      cross_domain_range: [68, 0.6, "d", "A"],
