      // [src_pamuk_srb, src_pamuk_aa, src_pamuk_nobel_presentation | 1990-2012 | critical essay + press feature + award-body speech] Ley traces a shift from the visible formal gamesmanship of the earlier novels toward broad-canvas social realism, and Anadolu Agency documents a museum built in parallel with the novel of the same name, with each cabinet designed by him as a companion to a part of the book. The changes of mode are documented; whether they were deliberate experiments or simply changes of direction rests on the critic's reading.
      experimentation: [64, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_pamuk_aa, src_pamuk_nobel_presentation, src_pamuk_nobel_bio | 1990s-2012 | press feature + award-body speech + self-report] Besides novels he built a physical museum and designed its 83 cabinets (Anadolu Agency), set My Name is Red among Ottoman miniature painters (Swedish Academy), and says he painted until 22 and studied architecture before writing (self-report). The museum and the painters' theme are independently documented; the earlier painting and architecture rest on his own statement.
      cross_domain_range: [64, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_pamuk_mookse, src_pamuk_aa | 2008-2016 | literary blog review + press feature] Monks describes a writer who 'obsesses' over detail to contain a world, and Anadolu Agency reports that Pamuk collected objects over years and personally designed each of 83 cabinets, some still waiting for the right object. Two sources of different kinds agree, but the first is one reviewer's impression of one novel. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): reviewed as a top-risk row; lowered from 66 / 0.50 to 62 / 0.46 because the prose-detail claim rests on a single blog reviewer and the museum evidence is curatorial rather than a measure of precision.
      detail_orientation: [62, 0.46, "s", "A"],
      // [src_pamuk_srb, src_pamuk_parisreview, src_pamuk_nobel_presentation | 1990s-2016 | critical essay + self-report interview + award-body speech] In the Paris Review he says he rejected the social-realist model of the previous generation of Turkish authors, and Ley quotes his essay saying a foreign view helps him 'fend off narrow nationalism and pressures to conform' and reads his ambiguous stance as a way of keeping his writing from being constrained by any viewpoint. The stance is literary and is documented mostly in his own words; the critic's reading is interpretation, and the Swedish Academy describes his refusal to judge his characters. Political statements and the 2005 charge are not scored. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): reviewed as a top-risk row; lowered from 66 / 0.50 to 62 / 0.46 because the main support is his own account of his stance and one critic's interpretation of it.
